1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,960
Scrape your knee, and within days new skin

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:03,920
has filled the gap.

3
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:07,040
Each new skin cell needs the same instructions

4
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:10,640
as the cells beside it, 46 chromosomes, the

5
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:11,840
DNA packages.

6
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:15,440
Copying DNA is only half the job, because

7
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:17,760
the copies still have to be shared out.

8
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:20,080
How does a cell divide so that both

9
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:23,280
new cells keep every chromosome, an exact copy

10
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:24,240
of the parent's?

11
00:00:25,467 --> 00:00:29,306
Your DNA is packed into structures called chromosomes,

12
00:00:29,427 --> 00:00:32,906
each one, before copying, a single long DNA

13
00:00:32,947 --> 00:00:35,386
molecule wound up with proteins.

14
00:00:35,707 --> 00:00:38,266
A human body cell has 46 of them,

15
00:00:38,347 --> 00:00:41,066
in two sets of 23, one set from

16
00:00:41,147 --> 00:00:42,506
each parent.

17
00:00:42,587 --> 00:00:44,426
A set is one of each kind of

18
00:00:44,507 --> 00:00:45,626
chromosome.

19
00:00:45,747 --> 00:00:48,506
So every kind appears twice, apart from X

20
00:00:48,587 --> 00:00:50,746
and Y in males, and the two of

21
00:00:50,827 --> 00:00:53,466
one kind, one from each parent, are called

22
00:00:53,547 --> 00:00:54,906
a homologous pair.

23
00:00:56,134 --> 00:00:58,453
A cell with two sets of chromosomes is

24
00:00:58,534 --> 00:00:59,813
called diploid.

25
00:01:00,054 --> 00:01:02,133
A cell with one set, like a human

26
00:01:02,214 --> 00:01:04,293
sperm or egg, is haploid.

27
00:01:04,854 --> 00:01:06,933
To keep the picture clear, we'll follow a

28
00:01:07,014 --> 00:01:10,213
model cell with just four chromosomes, two sets

29
00:01:10,294 --> 00:01:11,013
of two.

30
00:01:12,234 --> 00:01:13,353
Here is the problem.

31
00:01:13,834 --> 00:01:16,473
A chromosome can't be cut in half, because

32
00:01:16,554 --> 00:01:18,633
each half would carry only half of its

33
00:01:19,034 --> 00:01:19,993
instructions.

34
00:01:20,194 --> 00:01:21,913
So if a cell just shared out its

35
00:01:22,034 --> 00:01:24,873
chromosomes, each new cell would get only half

36
00:01:24,954 --> 00:01:27,113
of them, and a skin cell needs both

37
00:01:27,194 --> 00:01:28,313
sets.

38
00:01:28,394 --> 00:01:30,873
The DNA has to be copied first, as

39
00:01:30,954 --> 00:01:32,953
you saw in the DNA copying video.

40
00:01:34,167 --> 00:01:37,126
After copying, each chromosome is made of two

41
00:01:37,207 --> 00:01:39,926
identical copies, joined at a point called the

42
00:01:40,007 --> 00:01:40,726
centromere.

43
00:01:41,287 --> 00:01:43,846
Each copy is called a chromatid, and the

44
00:01:43,927 --> 00:01:46,166
two together are sister chromatids.

45
00:01:46,647 --> 00:01:49,206
It is still one chromosome, so to count

46
00:01:49,287 --> 00:01:53,126
chromosomes, count the centromeres, not the chromatids.

47
00:01:54,334 --> 00:01:56,093
When does the copying happen?

48
00:01:56,334 --> 00:01:59,813
In interphase, the long stage between divisions.

49
00:02:00,014 --> 00:02:01,453
It is not a rest.

50
00:02:01,854 --> 00:02:04,333
The cell grows and makes what it needs,

51
00:02:04,414 --> 00:02:06,493
and in a part called S phase it

52
00:02:06,574 --> 00:02:08,493
copies all of its DNA.

53
00:02:08,814 --> 00:02:12,013
So by the time mitosis starts, every chromosome

54
00:02:12,174 --> 00:02:14,173
already has two chromatids.

55
00:02:14,814 --> 00:02:17,533
Mitosis is the much shorter stage that shares

56
00:02:17,614 --> 00:02:18,573
those copies out.

57
00:02:19,134 --> 00:02:22,413
DNA is never copied during mitosis itself.

58
00:02:23,634 --> 00:02:25,713
Mitosis has four steps.

59
00:02:25,954 --> 00:02:29,633
In prophase, the long, thin chromosomes coil up

60
00:02:29,714 --> 00:02:32,513
into short, thick X shapes, which makes them

61
00:02:32,594 --> 00:02:35,513
easier to move, and the nuclear envelope, the

62
00:02:35,554 --> 00:02:38,393
membrane around the nucleus, breaks down.

63
00:02:38,754 --> 00:02:42,193
Spindle fibres, thin protein threads, grow from the

64
00:02:42,274 --> 00:02:43,393
two ends of the cell.

65
00:02:44,274 --> 00:02:47,233
In metaphase, fibres from the two ends attach

66
00:02:47,354 --> 00:02:50,513
to every chromosome at its centromere, one end

67
00:02:50,594 --> 00:02:53,313
to each chromatid, and the chromosomes line up

68
00:02:53,394 --> 00:02:54,753
across the middle.

69
00:02:54,834 --> 00:02:57,473
Each chromosome lines up on its own, not

70
00:02:57,554 --> 00:02:59,153
next to its homologous partner.

71
00:03:00,367 --> 00:03:03,726
In anaphase, the centromeres split and the fibres

72
00:03:03,887 --> 00:03:06,926
shorten, pulling the sister chromatids to opposite ends

73
00:03:07,007 --> 00:03:07,966
of the cell.

74
00:03:08,287 --> 00:03:10,606
As soon as a chromatid is separated, it

75
00:03:10,687 --> 00:03:12,446
is called a chromosome.

76
00:03:12,767 --> 00:03:15,086
So for a short time the whole cell

77
00:03:15,167 --> 00:03:17,966
holds twice as many chromosomes as before.

78
00:03:18,287 --> 00:03:20,526
But look at each end: it holds one

79
00:03:20,607 --> 00:03:22,926
copy of each of the four chromosomes the

80
00:03:23,007 --> 00:03:24,606
cell started with.

81
00:03:24,727 --> 00:03:26,606
Each end has both sets again.

82
00:03:27,834 --> 00:03:30,873
In telophase, the chromosomes reach the ends and

83
00:03:30,954 --> 00:03:34,233
uncoil into long thin threads again, and a

84
00:03:34,314 --> 00:03:36,793
new nuclear envelope forms around each group.

85
00:03:37,514 --> 00:03:41,433
Cytokinesis, the splitting of the cytoplasm, often begins

86
00:03:41,514 --> 00:03:42,873
while the nuclei form.

87
00:03:43,434 --> 00:03:46,233
In an animal cell, the membrane pinches in.

88
00:03:46,794 --> 00:03:49,273
Now there are two cells, each with both

89
00:03:49,354 --> 00:03:50,073
sets.

90
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:53,460
Let's track the numbers in our model cell,

91
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:55,660
which starts with four chromosomes.

92
00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:59,060
After copying, it still has four chromosomes, because

93
00:03:59,140 --> 00:04:02,740
we count centromeres, but now eight chromatids.

94
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:06,740
In anaphase, each chromatid becomes a chromosome, so

95
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:09,540
the cell briefly holds eight, four at each

96
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:09,940
end.

97
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:13,300
After the cell divides, each new cell holds

98
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:13,780
four.

99
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:17,140
A human cell goes the same way: 46

100
00:04:17,220 --> 00:04:21,700
chromosomes, and after copying 92 chromatids, because twice

101
00:04:21,780 --> 00:04:23,300
46 is 92.

102
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:27,460
In anaphase there are 92 chromosomes, 46 at

103
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:30,420
each end, then 46 in each new cell.

104
00:04:30,980 --> 00:04:32,740
The number is never halved.

105
00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:35,700
The copying doubled the DNA first, and the

106
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:37,540
division shared it back out.

107
00:04:38,767 --> 00:04:40,926
Why are the two new cells identical?

108
00:04:41,407 --> 00:04:44,046
Because sister chromatids are copies of the same

109
00:04:44,127 --> 00:04:44,846
chromosome.

110
00:04:45,327 --> 00:04:47,726
The fibres pull the two chromatids of every

111
00:04:47,807 --> 00:04:50,926
chromosome to opposite ends, so each new cell

112
00:04:51,007 --> 00:04:53,246
gets a copy of every chromosome the parent

113
00:04:53,327 --> 00:04:54,766
had, both sets.

114
00:04:55,327 --> 00:04:57,806
The cell also checks that every chromosome is

115
00:04:57,887 --> 00:05:01,086
attached from both ends before anaphase starts.

116
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:05,100
Mitosis is accurate, not perfect.

117
00:05:05,580 --> 00:05:07,980
A mistake made while copying the DNA is

118
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:09,140
a mutation.

119
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:10,780
It ends up in one of the two

120
00:05:10,860 --> 00:05:13,500
new cells, and then in every cell that

121
00:05:13,580 --> 00:05:14,700
divides from that one.

122
00:05:15,340 --> 00:05:18,460
Rarely, the two chromatids of one chromosome both

123
00:05:18,540 --> 00:05:20,620
move to the same end of the cell,

124
00:05:20,740 --> 00:05:23,020
so one new cell gets an extra chromosome

125
00:05:23,260 --> 00:05:25,100
and the other is one short.

126
00:05:25,420 --> 00:05:28,060
Checks catch many of these, but not all.

127
00:05:29,267 --> 00:05:32,546
Because every new cell gets the same chromosomes,

128
00:05:32,627 --> 00:05:35,266
mitosis is how an organism grows from one

129
00:05:35,347 --> 00:05:38,386
cell, how a graze heals, and how worn-out

130
00:05:38,467 --> 00:05:40,466
skin and gut lining are replaced.

131
00:05:41,027 --> 00:05:43,186
It is also how yeast and an amoeba

132
00:05:43,347 --> 00:05:44,866
make identical offspring.

133
00:05:45,507 --> 00:05:48,466
Bacteria have no nucleus, so they split by

134
00:05:48,547 --> 00:05:50,466
fission without mitosis.

135
00:05:51,667 --> 00:05:54,066
Two questions, both on screen.

136
00:05:54,387 --> 00:05:57,186
One: a fruit fly body cell has 8

137
00:05:57,267 --> 00:05:59,586
chromosomes, in two sets.

138
00:05:59,667 --> 00:06:02,706
Its DNA has just been copied, but mitosis

139
00:06:02,827 --> 00:06:04,306
has not started.

140
00:06:04,387 --> 00:06:06,866
How many chromosomes and chromatids does it hold

141
00:06:06,947 --> 00:06:09,746
now, and how many chromosomes does each new

142
00:06:09,827 --> 00:06:12,146
cell hold after mitosis?

143
00:06:12,627 --> 00:06:16,546
Two: this body cell is part-way through mitosis.

144
00:06:16,627 --> 00:06:19,266
Name the stage and count the chromosomes.

145
00:06:19,427 --> 00:06:21,426
Assume no mistakes.

146
00:06:21,587 --> 00:06:22,786
Pause here to answer.

147
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:33,600
One.

148
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:37,680
After copying, the cell still has 8 chromosomes,

149
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:40,640
because the copies stay joined at their centromeres,

150
00:06:40,680 --> 00:06:43,520
but it now has 16 chromatids.

151
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:46,800
After mitosis, each new cell has 8, the

152
00:06:46,920 --> 00:06:48,320
same as the parent.

153
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:51,040
If you said 4, you halved the number,

154
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:53,600
but the copying doubled the DNA first.

155
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:55,200
Two.

156
00:06:55,440 --> 00:06:58,080
The chromosomes are lined up across the middle,

157
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:01,120
with fibres attached from both ends, so this

158
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:02,400
is metaphase.

159
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:05,680
Count the centromeres: there are 6, so the

160
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:07,680
cell holds 6 chromosomes.

161
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:11,360
If you said 12, you counted chromatids, the

162
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:13,280
two copies in each X.

163
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:17,220
To keep the order, use I P M

164
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:18,020
A T.

165
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:21,940
I is interphase, which comes before mitosis and

166
00:07:22,020 --> 00:07:23,700
is where the DNA is copied.

167
00:07:24,180 --> 00:07:27,620
Prophase coils the chromosomes, metaphase lines them up

168
00:07:27,660 --> 00:07:30,500
in the middle, anaphase pulls them apart, and

169
00:07:30,580 --> 00:07:32,980
telophase finishes with two nuclei.

170
00:07:33,460 --> 00:07:35,620
Cytokinesis then splits the cell.

171
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:39,300
And to count chromosomes, count the centromeres, not

172
00:07:39,380 --> 00:07:40,540
the chromatids.

173
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:43,540
Next: meiosis, where a cell has to end

174
00:07:43,620 --> 00:07:45,620
up with one set, not two.

175
00:07:46,834 --> 00:07:48,273
Here are the key notes.

176
00:07:48,514 --> 00:07:50,433
Pause the video if you need more time

177
00:07:50,514 --> 00:07:51,313
to copy them.
